#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134088 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,z={t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\cdot {t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =({t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & ={t}^{5}+2{t}^{5}+3{t}^{5} \\\\  & =6{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={{t}^{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134089 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t ∈ [0, π]$." :question/worked-solution "The field has potential $\\phi=(x^2+y^2+z^2)/2$. At the initial point, $\\phi=1/2$; at $t=\\pi$, $\\phi=(1+4\\pi)/2$. Hence $$\\int_C F\\cdot dr=\\phi(r(\\pi))-\\phi(r(0))=2\\pi.$$ Equivalently $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)=2$ for $t>0$; its integral has the same finite limit at $t=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134090 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134091 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that  $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [1,2].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =1+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+4-(1+1)] \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134092 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair “$P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=-x$” does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair “$P(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{2}$” does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair “$P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$” gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134093 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,{x}^{2}+x⟩$ does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x+2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{4}+x,\\,\\frac{3x}{4}⟩$ gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{3}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,0⟩$ gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{4}+x,\\,\\frac{3x}{4}⟩$; $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,0⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134094 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$, what is the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$?" :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle=\\langle xy,y\\rangle$, the scalar curl is $Q_x-P_y=0-x=-x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134095 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}(xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(\\pi ,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\pi ,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(\\pi ,0)} & =-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0-\\pi \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134097 :question/problem "![](images/32/320a45751b2e718c66bb721cfcd78a2d206f08a9998fbbffddb1581ee995cf34.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}x\\,dy-y\\,dx,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x\\,dy-y\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-(-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (16-4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134098 :question/problem "![](images/72/7264984a1c19c247c030feae04615bb0c46205ca70475db1240db964ad6007b2.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=10,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-4,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/0d/0de833fcc39b6295418c1558cf1187774db7b4ada1d697f2dcac8c35858563df.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =10-4-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134099 :question/problem "![](images/52/52815b087f869769950d37ab6b95edb75accf030e742ed16b4679b6a41d7d638.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134100 :question/problem "![](images/52/52815b087f869769950d37ab6b95edb75accf030e742ed16b4679b6a41d7d638.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=1$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi -\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134101 :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bda5a10df8a1ec081db97e7aa52755796f1d50be5447e3e64f55986116ada6e.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{y}^{2})-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le 4,-3\\le y\\le 3\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-(-4))\\cdot (9-(-9))-(2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 18-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =138.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134102 :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbe865077b446bdd97c290cef0924d3f17807a9e1039c9be45f4aa6e4b29cc3c.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=1,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=5,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fd/fd221ae68fc4983fb0a3f7a745154764e5c0081b2b9b66e7f8ad4a150a8b3bc5.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the inner curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are opposite to those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =1-5-(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134129 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(1-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=1-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))+(-\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t))\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)+0\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-\\pi {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(\\pi t)-\\pi {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi )\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\pi [t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\pi (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134130 :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2y⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-2y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-2y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-2y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-2y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)+(-2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-palne is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot [{r}^{2}]_{r=0}^{r=1} \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134131 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x+y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨-2z,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-2z,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩{|}_{z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨-2(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+0)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c181e209cca01a1d6e372caa7f7810a5fa4aa9f177595bd92f937a7cba8b8a75.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134132 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=t-{t}^{3},\\,z=2{t}^{2}-2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(t-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =((t-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(t-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (1-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =t-4{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-4{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134133 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{3}-t,\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-x)\\,i \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t-{t}^{2}+t)\\,i \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,i\\,)\\cdot (\\,(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k\\,) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)+0\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}-{t}^{3}-2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\,2{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{3})-(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134134 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134135 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x = 1$, $x = 3$, $y =-1$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The divergence is\n$$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\partial(xz)}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial(y^2)}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial1}{\\partial z}=z+2y.$$\nThe Divergence Theorem therefore makes the missing integrand $z+2y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134136 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}y-2z}}}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134137 :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}4\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\hspace{0.167em}000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134138 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=⟨{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2y,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0+2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}2\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134139 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=2xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2) \\\\  & =2z+0+0 \\\\  & =2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}2z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134140 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134212 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=2+t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(3t)^{2}(2+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}(2+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =((18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =54{t}^{2}+27{t}^{3}+3t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}54{t}^{2}+27{t}^{3}+3t+1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={[18{t}^{3}+\\frac{27}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(18+\\frac{27}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{109}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134213 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for $x\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(1+{1}^{2}+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(2+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(2+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134236 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{3}-2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}t+{t}^{3}-2⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}\\cdot ({\\pi }^{3}-3)-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134276 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized \n by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}-t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{2}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}-t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}+t,$ and $y={t}^{2}-t.$ \n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t+1)^{2}+(2t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4t+1+4{t}^{2}-4t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\sqrt{2}[({t}^{2}+t)-({t}^{2}-t)] \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2\\sqrt{2}t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134277 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = 3x$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{4{t}^{3/2}}{3},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-t⟩\\hspace{0.167em}\n$$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$." :question/worked-solution "For a scalar line integral, substitute the parametrization and multiply by speed. Here $r'(t)=\\langle2\\sqrt t,t-1\\rangle$, so $\\|r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{4t+(t-1)^2}=t+1$ on $[0,1]$. Also $3x=4t^{3/2}$. Thus $$\\int_C3x\\,ds=4\\int_0^1(t^{5/2}+t^{3/2})\\,dt=4\\left(\\frac27+\\frac25\\right)=\\frac{96}{35}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134338 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{2} + y^{2})ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}t+t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3(\\operatorname{sin}t-t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}$ for $0 \\le t \\le 2π$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating gives $r'(t)=3t\\langle\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle$, so the speed is $3t$ on $[0,2\\pi]$. Also $x^2+y^2=9(1+t^2)$. Hence $$\\int_C(x^2+y^2)\\,ds=27\\int_0^{2\\pi}(t+t^3)\\,dt=54\\pi^2+108\\pi^4=54\\pi^2(1+2\\pi^2).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134367 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}y,{x}^{2}-2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f75ee554c61dc799273c2c731d608766d27987937d0058df6ee1e0290d8b060.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi \\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\pi x+2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\pi {x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{3}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134368 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{F}=2x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134369 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}\\pi z+2xz)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nEvaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}\\pi z+2xz) \\\\  & =yz-{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi x+\\pi {e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi z+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F})(1,0,0) & =(0)(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi +\\pi {e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}0+2(1) \\\\  & =0-(-1)+\\pi (1)+2 \\\\  & =1+\\pi +2 \\\\  & =\\pi +3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134370 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$, what is $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$?" :question/worked-solution "Using $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\langle R_y-Q_z,P_z-R_x,Q_x-P_y\\rangle$ gives\n$$\\nabla\\times\\langle xy,yz,xz\\rangle=\\langle-y,-z,-x\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134371 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=x\\operatorname{cos}yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\operatorname{ln}xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1, \\frac{π}{2}, 1)$." :question/worked-solution "The source asks for curl. For $\\mathbf F=\\langle x\\cos(yz),0,y\\ln(xz)\\rangle$, where $xz>0$,\n$$\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\ln(xz),-xy\\sin(yz)-\\frac yx,xz\\sin(yz)\\right\\rangle.$$\nAt $(1,\\pi/2,1)$ this becomes $\\langle0,-\\pi,1\\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134375 :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,3)$ and $(-1,3),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$  where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨3xy,{x}^{2}y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f831b2b707e63e8eb34ff5f6c1c45d0267f51001de2560834d212506b48ee41.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}3xy\\,dx+{x}^{2}y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}3xy\\,dx+{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(2y-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134376 :question/problem "![](images/df/df940d9ea08ed1b326d4904933bd41c318112836d2d657abcb3c158aabbe7bb4.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{{u}^{3}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134377 :question/problem "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}t}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
